### Detailed Technical Study: Object Sorting using Comparable and Comparator

#### 1. Why Two Sorting Interfaces Exist:
- **`Comparable`:** Used when an object has an intrinsic, default order (e.g. `Integer` naturally sorts by value; `String` naturally sorts alphabetically). The class implements `Comparable<T>` and overrides `compareTo()`.
- **`Comparator`:** Used when we need to sort objects by multiple criteria without modifying the source class, or when sorting third-party library classes whose code cannot be edited.

#### 2. Detailed Technical Comparison Table:
| Comparison Parameter | `Comparable<T>` Interface | `Comparator<T>` Interface |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Location** | `java.lang.Comparable` | `java.util.Comparator` |
| **Signature** | `public int compareTo(T o)` | `public int compare(T o1, T o2)` |
| **Class Coupling** | Tightly coupled; modifies source class. | Completely decoupled; implemented externally or via lambda. |
| **Number of Orders** | Exactly **one** natural ordering. | **Unlimited** custom sort strategies. |
| **Null Comparison** | Requires non-null instance. | Supports `Comparator.nullsFirst()` / `nullsLast()`. |

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation:
```java
import java.util.*;

// Class implementing Comparable for Natural Sorting by Roll Number
class Student implements Comparable<Student> {
    private int rollNo;
    private String name;
    private double marks;

    public Student(int rollNo, String name, double marks) {
        this.rollNo = rollNo;
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    public int getRollNo() { return rollNo; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public double getMarks() { return marks; }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        // Natural Sort: Ascending Roll Number
        return Integer.compare(this.rollNo, other.rollNo);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return String.format("[%03d] %-10s : %.1f%%", rollNo, name, marks);
    }
}

public class SortingComparisonDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();
        students.add(new Student(105, "Rahul", 88.5));
        students.add(new Student(101, "Anita", 95.0));
        students.add(new Student(103, "Deepak", 76.2));
        students.add(new Student(102, "Fatima", 92.4));

        // 1. Natural Sort via Comparable (Roll Number)
        Collections.sort(students);
        System.out.println("--- Sorted by Natural Order (Comparable: Roll No) ---");
        students.forEach(System.out::println);

        // 2. Custom Sort via Comparator: By Name Alphabetical
        students.sort(new Comparator<Student>() {
            @Override
            public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
                return s1.getName().compareTo(s2.getName());
            }
        });
        System.out.println("\n--- Sorted by Custom Order (Comparator: Name Alphabetical) ---");
        students.forEach(System.out::println);

        // 3. Custom Sort via Comparator: By Marks Descending (Lambda Syntax)
        students.sort((s1, s2) -> Double.compare(s2.getMarks(), s1.getMarks()));
        System.out.println("\n--- Sorted by Custom Order (Comparator: Marks Rank) ---");
        students.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Comparable handles default entity ordering, while Comparator provides dynamic multi-attribute sorting.}$$
